WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0% line:0%
Transcriber: 

00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:08.713
So today I'm going to talk
about the word goodwill.

00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:12.801
Goodwill, did you know,
is a technical accounting term.

00:00:13.301 --> 00:00:14.719
It's an asset

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:18.098
that's literally only created
when a buyer shows up

00:00:18.098 --> 00:00:20.684
and pays a purchase price for a company

00:00:20.725 --> 00:00:24.729
that's more than the fair value
of that company's net assets.

00:00:24.771 --> 00:00:29.317
So this unexplained differential
is named goodwill.

00:00:29.943 --> 00:00:34.447
And with it, accountants actually
can make balance sheets balance.

00:00:35.198 --> 00:00:38.159
Now, of course, this isn't how
most of us think of goodwill.

00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:41.663
We experience the more human
definition of goodwill.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.416
And I remember very clearly

00:00:44.416 --> 00:00:47.210
when that definition
first entered my life.

00:00:47.544 --> 00:00:49.838
It was 1976,

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:51.423
and I was five years old.

00:00:51.423 --> 00:00:54.426
And I was sitting there one morning
in kindergarten class

00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.513
when in walked the father
and older siblings of one of my friends.

00:00:59.806 --> 00:01:01.975
And they handed me a present.

00:01:01.975 --> 00:01:04.728
They gave me a little toy red helicopter,

00:01:04.728 --> 00:01:06.521
that sort of looked like this.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.440
And in the moment,

00:01:08.481 --> 00:01:11.192
I didn't really understand
why they were giving it to me.

00:01:11.192 --> 00:01:13.862
I just remember sensing
that they were happy

00:01:13.903 --> 00:01:15.572
and sad at the same time.

00:01:16.698 --> 00:01:20.285
And it wasn't only until much later
that I had my aha moment.

00:01:20.869 --> 00:01:23.496
That my friend had lost his mom recently,

00:01:23.538 --> 00:01:26.791
and that this gift, it was a thank you,

00:01:26.791 --> 00:01:29.502
because I would often
share my lunch with my friend

00:01:29.544 --> 00:01:31.671
on the many days
that he would come to school

00:01:31.671 --> 00:01:33.590
without any lunch of his own.

00:01:34.424 --> 00:01:35.967
And over time, as things happen,

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:38.637
I lost the toy,

00:01:38.678 --> 00:01:42.390
but I never forgot the lesson
and especially how it made me feel.

00:01:42.974 --> 00:01:47.812
My friend's father had rewarded kindness
without cheapening it,

00:01:47.812 --> 00:01:50.357
and he made it tangible with an object,

00:01:50.357 --> 00:01:53.109
and he made it human in that exchange,

00:01:53.151 --> 00:01:55.820
and he made it shareable with this story.

00:01:56.446 --> 00:02:00.992
And in doing all of this,
he created real value out of thin air

00:02:01.034 --> 00:02:03.870
by turning kindness into a scalable,

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:07.248
collectively-owned asset called goodwill.

00:02:08.583 --> 00:02:11.461
You know, and then for us,
for many of us, my life,

00:02:11.503 --> 00:02:13.505
the years just pass at a dizzying pace

00:02:13.546 --> 00:02:16.174
and I collected a couple of fancy degrees,

00:02:16.216 --> 00:02:18.718
I became a dad myself three times over.

00:02:19.177 --> 00:02:22.681
And I found myself wheeling and dealing
as an investment banker

00:02:22.681 --> 00:02:25.600
and a private equity investor
for many years.

00:02:26.142 --> 00:02:28.603
And the lessons from that red helicopter,

00:02:28.645 --> 00:02:32.232
they seemed incredibly childish
and weirdly misguided

00:02:32.232 --> 00:02:34.025
in the world of business and finance.

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:35.568
Because let’s face it:

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.571
non-revenue-generating
investments in people,

00:02:38.613 --> 00:02:41.700
they're generally not measured,
let alone rewarded.

00:02:42.325 --> 00:02:46.246
But something happened
in the summer of 2013

00:02:46.287 --> 00:02:49.416
that would forever change the way
I thought about kindness,

00:02:49.416 --> 00:02:52.836
goodwill and their roles
in reshaping business.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.507
I was on a series
of just deflating board calls

00:02:57.507 --> 00:03:01.428
involving a failed investment
in a company called Ashley Stewart.

00:03:01.845 --> 00:03:04.097
Ashley Stewart as a clothing retail

00:03:04.139 --> 00:03:09.102
that has served and employed
primarily plus-sized,

00:03:09.144 --> 00:03:12.313
moderate-income Black women

00:03:12.355 --> 00:03:15.191
in neighborhoods
across America since 1991.

00:03:16.151 --> 00:03:19.320
And I felt accountable
to my former employer,

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.197
to my former investors,

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:24.325
to the 1,000 plus employees
at Ashley Stewart,

00:03:24.367 --> 00:03:26.286
and frankly, to myself

00:03:26.327 --> 00:03:29.998
because I had saved this company
from a near-death bankruptcy filing

00:03:30.039 --> 00:03:32.083
just three years prior.

00:03:32.542 --> 00:03:34.836
So this time I did something,
I took an action,

00:03:34.836 --> 00:03:36.004
I made a choice,

00:03:36.045 --> 00:03:38.673
I resigned as chairman of the board

00:03:38.673 --> 00:03:42.051
and I agreed to serve as a first-time CEO
of this broken company.

00:03:42.802 --> 00:03:45.013
But I agreed to do it just for six months.

00:03:45.013 --> 00:03:48.141
I just wanted to avert
a nasty liquidation.

00:03:48.516 --> 00:03:51.436
And then I wanted to come home
and get on with my life.

00:03:52.687 --> 00:03:55.899
I had immediate pangs of regret

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.318
during those first few weeks
at Ashley Stewart.

00:03:59.527 --> 00:04:02.030
The corporate headquarters
was a converted warehouse.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:04.574
I just remember there being a lot of bugs.

00:04:04.574 --> 00:04:07.619
There was no Wi-Fi at the headquarters,

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:09.913
and the stores didn't have computers.

00:04:10.497 --> 00:04:12.332
And because there was a lack of trust,

00:04:12.373 --> 00:04:16.294
there were vendors in the lobby
demanding to be paid in cash upfront.

00:04:16.795 --> 00:04:18.129
And because of them,

00:04:18.171 --> 00:04:20.298
a lot of my employees were scared.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.802
And so I ended up having to hire
an armed security guard to protect them.

00:04:24.928 --> 00:04:26.846
And I felt alone,

00:04:26.846 --> 00:04:29.808
like, on every dimension
you can think of --

00:04:29.808 --> 00:04:32.936
geography, industry,

00:04:32.977 --> 00:04:36.064
gender and, yeah, race.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:41.236
My first town hall,
it went something along these lines.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:43.947
"Hi, I'm James.

00:04:44.614 --> 00:04:46.491
I've never done this before.

00:04:47.408 --> 00:04:50.703
You know, I may be the least qualified
person to run this company.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.290
But I'm here and I'm willing to learn.

00:04:54.624 --> 00:04:58.378
Oh, and by the way, after this speech,
I’ve got to head out to the DC,” --

00:04:58.378 --> 00:05:01.214
that’s a distribution center, right --

00:05:01.256 --> 00:05:02.799
"I've got to head out to the DC,

00:05:02.799 --> 00:05:06.386
I've got to find stuff to sell
to fund payroll.

00:05:06.427 --> 00:05:07.971
But don't panic.

00:05:08.012 --> 00:05:12.684
Because I think that if we can center
kindness and math together,

00:05:12.684 --> 00:05:15.687
then maybe we can get out
of this together."

00:05:16.646 --> 00:05:20.066
And I don't know why I said some
of these things with my outside voice.

00:05:21.192 --> 00:05:22.402
(Laughter)

00:05:22.443 --> 00:05:24.779
I may have panicked just a little bit,

00:05:24.821 --> 00:05:26.406
but the words were true

00:05:26.406 --> 00:05:28.449
and they did come from my heart.

00:05:28.449 --> 00:05:29.742
And you know, what happened

00:05:29.784 --> 00:05:34.247
was that the people who really wanted
to solve these problems once and for all,

00:05:34.289 --> 00:05:35.456
they stepped up.

00:05:35.748 --> 00:05:37.500
And it was the women.

00:05:37.542 --> 00:05:42.046
It was the employees
and customers alike at those stores,

00:05:42.046 --> 00:05:45.383
in those neighborhoods
across America who came forth.

00:05:45.383 --> 00:05:49.971
And they offered me insight after insight
as we work side by side together.

00:05:49.971 --> 00:05:53.349
And some of the customers,
sensing that we were in a lot of trouble,

00:05:53.391 --> 00:05:56.269
they even offered to bring back
their clothes hangers

00:05:56.269 --> 00:05:57.896
so that we could save money.

00:05:59.606 --> 00:06:01.649
You know, I know what you may be thinking,

00:06:01.691 --> 00:06:04.110
you might think, geez,
there were a lot of reasons

00:06:04.152 --> 00:06:07.989
why the ladies of Ashley Stewart
might have rejected me.

00:06:08.907 --> 00:06:10.116
But they didn't.

00:06:10.158 --> 00:06:11.826
They did the exact opposite.

00:06:12.243 --> 00:06:14.537
And store after store
sort of worked this way:

00:06:14.579 --> 00:06:16.748
They would say, “You’re James?

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:19.792
You’re the new CEO of Ashley Stewart?

00:06:20.668 --> 00:06:22.712
Well, OK, let’s go.”

00:06:23.338 --> 00:06:26.132
And this played out in town after town,

00:06:26.174 --> 00:06:27.508
city after city.

00:06:27.884 --> 00:06:30.595
And their generosity toward one another

00:06:30.637 --> 00:06:32.972
and their kindness to me,

00:06:33.014 --> 00:06:35.808
it just rekindled memories
of the red helicopter.

00:06:36.351 --> 00:06:38.561
And I remembered the lesson

00:06:38.603 --> 00:06:41.189
that goodwill, like, real goodwill,

00:06:41.189 --> 00:06:43.191
it is a real asset

00:06:43.232 --> 00:06:46.027
that can compound and be amplified

00:06:46.027 --> 00:06:48.363
independent of financial capital.

00:06:48.404 --> 00:06:50.573
So late, late nights --

00:06:50.573 --> 00:06:53.076
And I'm going to use these
words very deliberately --

00:06:53.117 --> 00:06:58.206
It inspired me to compose, draw, design,

00:06:58.206 --> 00:07:00.959
create a new business plan

00:07:00.959 --> 00:07:03.044
with new mathematical formulas,

00:07:03.044 --> 00:07:08.508
operational protocols that were woven
together with multi-sensory storytelling,

00:07:08.549 --> 00:07:12.345
whose singular purpose
was to create real value,

00:07:12.387 --> 00:07:14.430
driving real goodwill.

00:07:15.556 --> 00:07:19.560
The business plan fundamentally
had three concentric spirals

00:07:19.602 --> 00:07:20.770
underpinning it.

00:07:20.812 --> 00:07:21.938
So picture this.

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:23.189
So first,

00:07:23.231 --> 00:07:25.358
we had the courage

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:28.277
to establish a culture of kindness
in the workplace.

00:07:28.319 --> 00:07:29.779
Period, end of story.

00:07:30.154 --> 00:07:32.699
It was a strategic priority
day in, day out.

00:07:32.699 --> 00:07:35.785
And yeah, there were moments
as individuals we failed.

00:07:36.077 --> 00:07:37.453
But as a collective,

00:07:37.453 --> 00:07:40.164
we were very successful
in changing attitudes

00:07:40.206 --> 00:07:43.209
about the transformative power
of kindness at work.

00:07:43.876 --> 00:07:45.628
Because you see, what kindness does,

00:07:45.628 --> 00:07:48.297
it distributes the joy, actually,

00:07:48.339 --> 00:07:50.383
of problem-solving to everyone.

00:07:50.717 --> 00:07:54.595
It creates a safe environment
that unleashes innovation,

00:07:54.637 --> 00:07:56.639
especially the unselfish kind.

00:07:56.931 --> 00:08:01.310
And it turns perceived
liabilities into assets,

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.604
which, as any financial
accountant will tell you,

00:08:03.646 --> 00:08:06.024
mathematically has to result

00:08:06.024 --> 00:08:09.694
in the creation of real equity value
in every meaning of the word.

00:08:10.153 --> 00:08:11.320
Second,

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:14.782
once we felt sure about the authenticity

00:08:14.782 --> 00:08:17.869
of the internal goodwill
that we had co-created,

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:21.414
we just wanted to show the whole world
and make it tangible to everybody.

00:08:21.706 --> 00:08:25.084
So by trusting our employees
and our customers

00:08:25.126 --> 00:08:27.670
and by leveraging their smartphones

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:29.255
and their social media platforms,

00:08:29.297 --> 00:08:33.342
we were able to scale our marketing
creative efforts very fast

00:08:33.342 --> 00:08:35.553
in a very low-cost and organic way.

00:08:36.262 --> 00:08:38.639
We invited everyone in,

00:08:38.639 --> 00:08:41.100
and we were able to merge ecosystems

00:08:41.142 --> 00:08:44.228
and blurring the lines between
outsider and insider,

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:46.064
and them and us.

00:08:46.105 --> 00:08:48.941
And we were able to create a seat
at the table for everyone,

00:08:48.941 --> 00:08:51.402
which, by the way, enabled us
to leverage the assets

00:08:51.402 --> 00:08:53.279
and the balance sheets of everyone.

00:08:53.905 --> 00:08:57.575
And third, once we centered goodwill,

00:08:57.617 --> 00:08:59.452
we had to center people.

00:08:59.452 --> 00:09:03.372
And it wasn't long before we realized
we had to redo our financial formulas

00:09:03.372 --> 00:09:04.832
and our financial reporting.

00:09:05.416 --> 00:09:08.002
So payroll, instead of being an expense,

00:09:08.044 --> 00:09:09.587
became an investment.

00:09:09.629 --> 00:09:11.130
And internal communications,

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.592
well, that was just a great way
to train and to teach.

00:09:14.592 --> 00:09:18.554
And the materials that we created
became critical first stanzas

00:09:18.554 --> 00:09:21.307
of the overall narrative
that was our brand song.

00:09:21.849 --> 00:09:24.268
And working capital?

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:27.605
Well, our vendors
became effectively co-investors,

00:09:27.647 --> 00:09:31.192
and we would measure
working capital as a unit of trust

00:09:31.192 --> 00:09:34.904
that we were rebuilding
together step by step.

00:09:35.279 --> 00:09:37.240
And finally, human resources.

00:09:37.657 --> 00:09:40.201
Well, how could that possibly
be a cost center?

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:44.455
See, when you center
on maximizing goodwill,

00:09:44.455 --> 00:09:46.999
it forces you to actually measure it.

00:09:47.583 --> 00:09:51.921
And you realize that despite
what the rules of accounting might say,

00:09:51.963 --> 00:09:56.259
that it's operations
and leadership and culture

00:09:56.259 --> 00:09:57.927
that actually create goodwill.

00:09:58.511 --> 00:10:01.097
Not some outside source
of financial capital.

00:10:01.097 --> 00:10:04.809
And it makes accounting
actually accountable.

00:10:05.476 --> 00:10:07.728
And it isn't long also before you realize

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:12.859
that the entire flow of your operations
has to naturally follow suit.

00:10:13.442 --> 00:10:14.861
So to illustrate all of this,

00:10:14.902 --> 00:10:18.698
I'm going to take you back
to the holiday season of 2013,

00:10:18.698 --> 00:10:23.202
which was only four months removed
from my sort of "meh" town hall.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:26.080
We were out of money at this point,

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:27.623
and I was terrified.

00:10:27.623 --> 00:10:31.127
But we made a couple of decisions
that seemed to have long-term importance

00:10:31.127 --> 00:10:32.587
for our efforts.

00:10:32.628 --> 00:10:35.047
We hosted a really modest holiday party

00:10:35.089 --> 00:10:37.842
at a homeless shelter for women
in Brooklyn, New York.

00:10:38.384 --> 00:10:40.803
And at this point,
we didn't have anything to give,

00:10:40.803 --> 00:10:42.430
so we gave our time.

00:10:42.471 --> 00:10:44.056
We gave our hearts,

00:10:44.056 --> 00:10:47.894
and we found a few boxes
of merchandise samples that we donated.

00:10:47.894 --> 00:10:49.979
And around that time as well,

00:10:50.021 --> 00:10:53.774
we organized a chain-wide
sales competition.

00:10:53.774 --> 00:10:56.068
The top 40 stores

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:59.864
earned the right to donate 250 dollars

00:10:59.906 --> 00:11:02.283
to a local charity of their own choosing.

00:11:03.075 --> 00:11:05.328
Together, these two things, right,

00:11:05.369 --> 00:11:09.040
the event -- the party, the competition,

00:11:09.081 --> 00:11:12.460
they were just a way for us to
say "thank you" and "goodbye"

00:11:12.501 --> 00:11:14.503
to the local neighborhoods and communities

00:11:14.545 --> 00:11:16.839
that had supported
this company for 20 years.

00:11:17.757 --> 00:11:20.718
And the outpouring was just amazing.

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:22.803
We saw real friendships

00:11:22.803 --> 00:11:24.889
that have been developed
over those 20 years

00:11:24.889 --> 00:11:26.557
play out right in front of us.

00:11:26.599 --> 00:11:28.893
And our newly installed e-com platform?

00:11:28.893 --> 00:11:32.188
We operated it in a way
that accentuated that

00:11:32.188 --> 00:11:35.066
and connected people,
rather than diminishing humanity.

00:11:35.816 --> 00:11:37.693
And sales just exploded.

00:11:38.069 --> 00:11:39.320
And just like that,

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:42.156
we had found a way
to operationalize goodwill.

00:11:42.573 --> 00:11:44.700
In our internal culture,

00:11:44.742 --> 00:11:46.911
in our outbound sales and marketing,

00:11:46.953 --> 00:11:50.665
but also, perhaps most significantly,
in our sense of compensation system.

00:11:50.706 --> 00:11:53.209
There were all three spirals
just sort of doing this,

00:11:53.209 --> 00:11:54.752
mutually reinforcing each other.

00:11:54.794 --> 00:11:56.170
We measured everything.

00:11:56.712 --> 00:12:00.091
You know, down the road,
as the strategy really took hold,

00:12:00.132 --> 00:12:04.095
and I was finally able to secure
some financial capital

00:12:04.095 --> 00:12:07.348
and make some necessary
technological investments,

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:09.016
we just did the same thing,

00:12:09.058 --> 00:12:11.477
just over and over again at scale.

00:12:11.477 --> 00:12:14.730
So we rolled out something
called a CEO Citizenship Award.

00:12:14.772 --> 00:12:16.440
That was the most prestigious,

00:12:16.482 --> 00:12:18.901
highest-compensated
bonus system in the company.

00:12:18.901 --> 00:12:22.196
It went to those employees
who lived out the following credo.

00:12:22.196 --> 00:12:23.864
You act like an owner.

00:12:23.864 --> 00:12:25.157
Be a good friend.

00:12:25.157 --> 00:12:26.575
Be a good mentor.

00:12:26.617 --> 00:12:30.413
We never factored in sales or revenues
into these determinations.

00:12:31.247 --> 00:12:33.916
Second, you know, our marketing team,

00:12:33.958 --> 00:12:35.668
our outbound-facing consumer team

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:39.964
continued to do most of the most important
internal communications.

00:12:40.423 --> 00:12:42.300
And I, as a former high school teacher,

00:12:42.341 --> 00:12:47.346
I relished the fact that I could teach
the principles of money, life and joy

00:12:47.388 --> 00:12:49.515
to my colleagues at scale.

00:12:49.974 --> 00:12:52.476
And that really kind of sad holiday party?

00:12:52.518 --> 00:12:55.313
Well that evolved into something big.

00:12:55.313 --> 00:12:57.315
We called it Finding Ashley Stewart,

00:12:57.356 --> 00:12:59.817
which was an annual, nationwide,

00:12:59.859 --> 00:13:01.444
year-long search

00:13:01.444 --> 00:13:03.946
for unsung women leaders
in our communities.

00:13:04.363 --> 00:13:08.159
It would culminate in front
of 3,000 screaming fans

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:11.704
at the Brooklyn Kings Theater
and a star-studded concert.

00:13:12.038 --> 00:13:14.665
And the best part of all of it
was that the vendors,

00:13:14.665 --> 00:13:17.376
which by now included some
of the world's leading tech

00:13:17.418 --> 00:13:19.003
and financial companies,

00:13:19.003 --> 00:13:21.172
they're the ones who funded all of this.

00:13:22.298 --> 00:13:27.136
You know, I ended up spending seven
years at Ashley Stewart, leading it.

00:13:27.553 --> 00:13:29.472
So, far from getting on with my life,

00:13:29.513 --> 00:13:32.183
it became part of my life,
especially the friendships.

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:35.811
And we had to make some
tough business decisions along the way,

00:13:35.811 --> 00:13:37.605
and not everyone made it.

00:13:37.605 --> 00:13:42.068
But our overall strategy and philosophy
generated staggering results.

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:45.529
And yes, it showed up
in our financial statements, too.

00:13:46.113 --> 00:13:48.157
So within two years,

00:13:48.157 --> 00:13:50.993
we generated more
in annual operating profit

00:13:51.035 --> 00:13:53.454
than we paid for all
of the assets of the company.

00:13:53.871 --> 00:13:55.247
And our digital strategy,

00:13:55.289 --> 00:13:59.126
which never forgot to center
the real friendships in the stores,

00:13:59.126 --> 00:14:01.170
it landed us on the stages

00:14:01.212 --> 00:14:04.298
of the most prestigious
business conferences in the world

00:14:04.298 --> 00:14:07.343
celebrating our growth and our innovation,

00:14:07.385 --> 00:14:10.388
only a few years removed
from not even having Wi-Fi.

00:14:10.721 --> 00:14:13.808
And it wasn't long before
the financial community,

00:14:13.808 --> 00:14:17.144
well, they changed their minds
and they showed up at our headquarters,

00:14:17.144 --> 00:14:18.979
but this time with their checkbooks,

00:14:18.979 --> 00:14:20.523
and they stroked us a check

00:14:20.564 --> 00:14:24.026
that made us convert our real goodwill

00:14:24.068 --> 00:14:27.905
into accounting goodwill to the tune
of well north of 100 million dollars.

00:14:28.572 --> 00:14:31.075
And I know that none of this
would have been possible

00:14:31.117 --> 00:14:33.828
without the lessons that I learned
from my friend's father

00:14:33.869 --> 00:14:35.996
way back in kindergarten.

00:14:36.038 --> 00:14:41.127
He invested dollars,
but also his time and his heart

00:14:41.127 --> 00:14:45.256
into a little toy red helicopter in 1976.

00:14:45.714 --> 00:14:47.550
And in the way that he did it,

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:50.761
it created a real asset called goodwill.

00:14:50.803 --> 00:14:54.473
And that goodwill compounded
quietly for decades

00:14:54.515 --> 00:14:57.226
and then ultimately helped save a company,

00:14:57.268 --> 00:14:59.186
around a thousand jobs,

00:14:59.186 --> 00:15:03.023
a safe place for a really
deserving group of women,

00:15:03.065 --> 00:15:06.986
and over 1 billion dollars
in overall economic productivity.

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:10.948
And in doing so,
he taught an enduring lesson

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:14.618
that helped this grown man
regain his balance,

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:16.078
a true balance,

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:18.247
by remembering to slow down

00:15:18.289 --> 00:15:22.209
and to see the world through the eyes
of a five-year-old child

00:15:22.251 --> 00:15:24.545
who knows unequivocally

00:15:24.587 --> 00:15:29.550
that there is one and only one
definition of goodwill

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:33.053
that creates real value in life

00:15:33.095 --> 00:15:35.514
and yeah, in business, too.

00:15:36.307 --> 00:15:38.058
Thank you very much for listening.

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.311
(Applause)

